    {
      "id": "s2016-e2-q10",
      "number": 10,
      "points": 8,
      "stem": "The derivative of the function $g(x)$ is $g'(x) = \\sqrt{\\sec^2 x\\tan^2 x - 1}$. What is the length of the curve $y = g(x)$ on the interval $0 \\le x \\le \\frac{\\pi}{4}$?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "text": "$\\sqrt{2} - 1$"
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "text": "$\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 1$"
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "text": "$\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$"
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "text": "$\\sqrt{2}$"
        },
        {
          "label": "E",
          "text": "$1$"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "A",
      "lessonIds": [
        "L8"
      ],
      "sourcePage": 7,
      "solution": "**Note on this question:** The given $g'(x)=\\sqrt{\\sec^2x\\tan^2x-1}$ is not a real number near $x=0$: the expression under the root is $-1$ at $x=0$ and stays negative until $x\\approx0.666$. Formally, $1+(g'(x))^2=\\sec^2x\\tan^2x$, so $L=\\int_0^{\\pi/4}\\sec x\\tan x\\,dx=\\sec\\frac{\\pi}{4}-\\sec 0=\\sqrt{2}-1$, the keyed answer A.",
      "status": "reviewed"
    },
